  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana, and the rule's first run was still pending when I created it, so neither has been checked beyond Kibana accepting them.\n\n**Data view:** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` fields that matter here:\n- `timestamp`: the time field.\n- `bytes`: response size.\n- `clientip`: the client.\n- `response`: the status code, stored as text. The aggregatable version is `response.keyword`.\n- `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `extension`, `url` and `referer`: breakdown fields.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-7b70abb0 Web logs overview\" (id `09350bb9-88cb-4a58-b04b-17ec9c1b32c7`) has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a count metric.\n- Average bytes.\n- Unique clients, counted by `clientip`.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart built on an ES|QL query.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-7b70abb0 5xx spike\" (id `fbcf45ae-4586-4797-a3d0-93ea64d0009d`) is an Elasticsearch query rule.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts documents in a 5-minute window where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- It fires when that count is greater than 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so it will raise alerts in Kibana but won't send notifications anywhere.",
